The operations team's two additions are not included. This node's `with` clause grants only `aap:caps/state-rw@0.1.0`. Writing to `/var/log/aap/node-inputs.log` would need a filesystem capability, and calling `metadata.internal.example` would need an `http-client` capability. Neither is in the node's authority, and there is no WASI adapter on this target anyway. Also, `PublishedDigest` has no text field to put in the `q=` parameter.

To get either feature, the graph has to declare it. That means adding the capability to this node's `with` clause, or better, adding a separate node that holds it.

The body records each considered item ID in the `seen` store, but only if the digest was actually published. If publishing failed, the items stay unseen so the next run can pick them up again.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{published-digest, run-report};

    import aap:caps/state-rw@0.1.0;

    export run: func(published: published-digest) -> run-report;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::state_rw;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(published: PublishedDigest) -> RunReport {
        let mut recorded: u32 = 0;

        // Only mark items seen once the digest actually went out, so a failed
        // publish leaves them eligible for the next run.
        if published.published {
            let mut done: Vec<&str> = Vec::new();
            for id in published.considered.iter() {
                let id = id.as_str();
                if id.is_empty() || done.contains(&id) {
                    continue;
                }
                state_rw::put(id, &published.date);
                done.push(id);
                recorded = recorded.saturating_add(1);
            }
        }

        RunReport {
            date: published.date,
            recorded,
        }
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

